import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class LexicographicalOrder {

    /**
     * Returns all integers in the range [1, n] in lexicographical order.
     *
     * @param n The upper limit of the range.
     * @return List of integers in lexicographical order.
     */
    public static List<Integer> lexicalOrder(int n) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        lexicalOrderHelper(result, 1, n);
        return result;
    }

    private static void lexicalOrderHelper(List<Integer> result, int current, int n) {
        if (current > n) return;
        result.add(current);
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            int next = current * 10 + i;
            if (next > n) break;
            lexicalOrderHelper(result, next, n);
        }
    }

    // Test cases to verify the solution
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(lexicalOrder(13)); // Expected: [1,10,11,12,13,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9]
        System.out.println(lexicalOrder(2)); // Expected: [1,2]
        System.out.println(lexicalOrder(100)); // Expected [1,10,11,12,...,19,2,20,21,...,99,100]
    }
}
```

This solution uses a helper method `lexicalOrderHelper` to recursively generate the lexicographical order. Starting from 1, it explores each digit from 0 to 9 to build the next number and adds it to the result list if it is within the bounds of the given number `n`. The main method includes test cases to verify the correctness of the solution.

